<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Behaviors</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.2.3" />

<p class="block-content">
A behavior is an object that adds properties, methods, and event responses to a
<tt>TComponent</tt> at runtime. The component keeps its own class, and the behavior
supplies the additional functionality. Any class derived from <tt>TComponent</tt>
accepts behaviors, so behaviors extend controls, modules, services, and the
application itself. Behaviors are attached and detached while the application runs,
which allows a component's capabilities to change without changing its class.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
PRADO provides two kinds of behavior. An object behavior attaches to one component
instance. A class behavior attaches to every instance of a class. Both derive from
the shared base class <tt>TBaseBehavior</tt> and share the same attach, enable, and
priority model.
</p>

<h2>Object behaviors</h2>
<p class="block-content">
An object behavior extends <tt>TBehavior</tt> and implements <tt>IBehavior</tt>. Each
<tt>TBehavior</tt> instance has one owner. Object behaviors hold per-instance state,
so a behavior may store data that belongs to its single owner. The behavior refers to
its owner through a weak reference, so the behavior does not prevent the owner from
being garbage-collected. A behavior reads its owner with <tt>getOwner</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
class TExtraPropertyBehavior extends TBehavior
{
    private $_extra = '';

    public function getExtra()
    {
        return $this->_extra;
    }

    public function setExtra($value)
    {
        $this->_extra = $value;
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Once attached, the owner exposes the behavior's public properties and methods as its
own. The owner reads <tt>$component->Extra</tt> and the call resolves to the attached
behavior.
</p>

<h2>Class behaviors</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A class behavior extends <tt>TClassBehavior</tt> and implements <tt>IClassBehavior</tt>.
One <tt>TClassBehavior</tt> instance serves every instance of a class at the same time,
so a class behavior is written to be stateless. When a component calls a class-behavior
method, PRADO prepends the calling component to the argument list. The method receives
the owner as its first parameter and therefore knows which instance is involved.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
class TAuditBehavior extends TClassBehavior
{
    // the owner is always the first parameter of a class-behavior method
    public function logSave($owner)
    {
        Prado::log('Saving ' . get_class($owner), TLogger::INFO, 'app.audit');
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Class behaviors are registered with the static method <tt>attachClassBehavior</tt> and
removed with <tt>detachClassBehavior</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
// attach to every current and future instance of TArticleRecord
TComponent::attachClassBehavior('audit', new TAuditBehavior(), TArticleRecord::class);

// remove the class behavior
TComponent::detachClassBehavior('audit', TArticleRecord::class);
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
A class behavior applies to instances created after it is registered. It also applies
to instances that already exist and listen to global events. Registering a class
behavior raises the global event <tt>fxAttachClassBehavior</tt>, and every listening
instance attaches the new behavior. Removing a class behavior raises
<tt>fxDetachClassBehavior</tt>, and every listening instance detaches it. An instance
begins listening after <tt>listen</tt> is called on it. The
<a href="?page=Fundamentals.Components2">Components: Events</a> page describes global
events.
</p>

<h2>Attaching and detaching behaviors</h2>
<p class="block-content">
An object behavior is attached to a component with <tt>attachBehavior</tt>, which takes
a name and the behavior. The name identifies the behavior for later enabling, disabling,
and detachment.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
$component->attachBehavior('extra', new TExtraPropertyBehavior());
$component->detachBehavior('extra');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Whole sets of behaviors are attached with <tt>attachBehaviors</tt> and removed with
<tt>detachBehaviors</tt>. <tt>clearBehaviors</tt> removes every behavior from a
component. Behavior names are case-insensitive.
</p>

<h2>Enabling and disabling behaviors</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A single behavior is turned on and off by name with <tt>enableBehavior</tt> and
<tt>disableBehavior</tt>. A disabled behavior stays attached, and its added properties,
methods, and event responses do not run until it is enabled again.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
$component->disableBehavior('extra');
$component->enableBehavior('extra');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Every behavior on a component is turned on and off together with
<tt>enableBehaviors</tt> and <tt>disableBehaviors</tt>. <tt>getBehaviorsEnabled</tt>
reports whether behaviors are currently enabled on the component.
</p>

<h2>Behavior priority</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Behaviors are stored in priority order. A lower priority number runs earlier, and the
default priority is 10. The priority is given as the third argument to
<tt>attachBehavior</tt> or as the fourth argument to <tt>attachClassBehavior</tt>. When
several behaviors respond to the same method or event, PRADO calls them in priority
order.
</p>

<h2>Identifying behaviors: asa and isa</h2>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>asa</tt> returns an attached behavior. The name reads as "as a". Given a behavior
name, <tt>asa</tt> returns that behavior or null.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
$behavior = $component->asa('extra');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>isa</tt> reports whether a component is an instance of a class. The name reads as
"is a". <tt>isa</tt> returns true when the component itself is an instance of the class,
and also when any attached behavior is an instance of the class. A behavior implements
<tt>IInstanceCheck</tt> to answer <tt>isa</tt> for a class the behavior represents. In
that case the host component reports itself as an instance of the class the behavior
supplies.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
When <tt>asa</tt> is called with a class name and no behavior of that name is found,
<tt>asa</tt> returns the first attached behavior that is an instance of the class.
<tt>getBehaviors</tt> returns the attached behaviors, and it accepts an optional class
name to return only the behaviors of that class.
</p>

<h2>Behaviors and dynamic events</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A behavior responds to a component's dynamic events by defining a method whose name
starts with <tt>dy</tt>. When the component calls a dynamic event, PRADO invokes the
matching method on each attached, enabled behavior in priority order through a
<tt>TCallChain</tt>. Each behavior returns a value and may pass a value to the next
behavior in the chain. Dynamic events let a behavior observe the component, filter
data, and decide whether a step runs. The
<a href="?page=Fundamentals.Components2">Components: Events</a> page describes global
events, dynamic events, and the call chain in full.
</p>

<h2>Loading behaviors from configuration</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />
<p class="block-content">
Behaviors are attached from the application configuration with
<tt>TBehaviorsModule</tt>, without writing code. A behavior element names the behavior,
its class, the target it attaches to, a priority, and the behavior's properties. PRADO
also ships ready-made behaviors under the <tt>Prado\Util\Behaviors</tt> namespace, such
as <tt>TParameterizeBehavior</tt>, which sets an owner property from an application
parameter. The <a href="?page=Configurations.BehaviorsConfig">Behaviors
Configuration</a> page covers the module, the behavior attributes, the ready-made
behaviors, and loading behaviors from parameters.
</p>

<h2>Behaviors update in 4.3.0</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
Behaviors are carried through cloning and serialization. When a component is cloned,
its behaviors are cloned and attached to the new instance. When a component is
serialized and unserialized, its behaviors are restored. On unserialize, a component
also gains any named class behaviors that were registered for its class while it was
stored.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>IBaseBehavior</tt> defines an <tt>init</tt> method that receives the behavior's
configuration, which a behavior overrides to read complex configuration. A behavior
declares its event handlers by returning name-to-handler pairs from <tt>events</tt>,
and a handler in that list may be a <tt>Closure</tt>. Registered event handlers attach
at the behavior's priority. Handlers are attached and detached automatically as the
behavior is enabled and disabled.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
A behavior may be attached without a name, in which case PRADO assigns it a numeric
name. A behavior that implements <tt>IBehavior</tt> is attached class-wide by cloning
one instance for each owner. Class behaviors attach to a class through its parents,
interfaces, and first-level traits, which <tt>getClassHierarchy</tt> reports.
</p>

</com:TContent>
